# EE282 Project Analysis Proposal
## Sebastian Gallego

## How do bees digest pollen?
Pollen feeding ("palynivory") is a trait that likely facilitated the evolution of bees from carnivorous wasp ancestors. Although palynivory is recognized as a key evolutionary innovation, we know surprisingly little about how bees digest pollen. Pollen nutrients are enclosed within a highly recalcitrant cell wall, presenting a formidable digestive challenge. One hypothesized mechanism involves plant cell wall-degrading enzymes (PCWDEs), which may weaken the cell wall and release nutrients. However, whether bees employ this mechanism remains largely untested. Comparative genomics provides an opportunity to investigate whether differences in feeding ecology between bees and wasps are associated with the evolution of digestive enzymes. Here, I aim to **(1) identify candidate plant cell wall-degrading enzymes (PCWDEs)** and **(2) compare their diversity and abundance between bees (n=5) and wasp (n=5) using genomic data**. I hypothesize that bee species will possess a greater diversity and abundance of PCWDEs than carnivorous wasps, reflecting their adaptation to pollen feeding. Including taxa from five major bee families will also shed light on whether particular PCWDE families are shared or taxon specific.

## Genomic Datasets 
For this project, I will analyze publicly available reference genomes from five bee species (_Bombus terrestris_, _Osmia lignaria_, _Halictus rubicundus_, _Colletes latitarsis_, and _Andrena cerasifolii_) and five wasp species (_Vespa crabro_, _Diachasmimorpha longicaudata_, _Nasonia vitripennis_, _Belonocnema kinseyi_, and _Vespula vulgaris_). These species represent nine hymenopteran families, providing a broad taxonomic representation for comparing diversity and abundance of PCWDEs. Genome assemblies were identified through NCBI GenBank and selected based on assembly completeness, assembly level, annotation availability, and taxonomy. All genomes are assembled at the chromosome level and have high BUSCO completeness scores (93.1-98.9%), evaluated using the same lineage dataset (hymenoptera_odb10). Additionally, all genomes have been annotated using NCBI's Eukaryotic Genome Annotation Pipeline (EGAP), with predicted protein sequences available. I will retrieve the protein FASTA files for each assembly and use them as input for carbohydrate-active enzyme (CAZyme) annotation and identification required for eukaryotic CAZyme analysis with dbCAN (Zheng et al. 2023). 

## Bioinformatic Workflow in HPC3 
In an effort to improve Bash scripting skills, all bioinformatic analyses will be conducted on UCI's High Performance Computing Cluster (HPC3). First, I will install NCBI Datasets CLI using conda and use it to download the protein FASTA files, GFF3 annotation files, and associated metadata for all ten species using their GenBank assembly accession numbers. Next, I will install dbCAN using Conda, download its reference databases, and identify CAZymes in each predicted proteome with the `run_dbcan CAZyme_annotation` command. To improve annotation confidence, I will only retain CAZymes identified by at least two of the three dbCAN annotation methods (HMMER, DIAMOND, and dbCAN-sub) following Zhang et al. (2018). Using the CAZy database and published literature (Shin et al. 2026, McKenna et al. 2019), I will manually curate a list of families that contain enzymes capable of degrading cellulose, hemicellulose, or pectin and classify them as candidate PCWDE families. I will then write a Bash script to filter the dbCAN results against the curated list, extracting the corresponding protein IDs and CAZyme family assignments for each species. Following filtering, I will cross-reference the protein IDs with their corresponding gene IDs in the GFF3 annotation files to ensure that multiple protein isoforms originating from the same gene are counted only once, avoiding the overestimation of PCWDE gene abundance. All software versions, reference database releases, and download dates will be documented to ensure the analysis is reproducible. 


## Analysis and Visualizations in R 
Following data curation, candidate PCWDE gene counts and CAZy family assignments will be imported into R for comparative analysis, providing an opportunity to apply and strengthen techniques learned in class. For each species, I will quantify PCWDE gene abundance as the total number of genes encoding candidate PCWDEs and family richness as the number of distinct CAZy families represented. I will use the R package pheatmap to construct a hierarchically clustered heatmap depicting the abundance of candidate PCWDE genes across CAZy families and species. This visualization will allow me to identify patterns of enzyme family abundance and determine whether bees share similar PCWDE repertoires that distinguish them from wasps. Additionally, I will use ggplot2 to generate boxplots displaying differences in total PCWDE gene abundance and CAZy family richness between bees and wasps, with individual species represented as points. I will use Wilcoxon rank-sum tests to evaluate whether these differences are statistically significant. Finally, I will generate stacked bar plots showing the distribution of candidate PCWDE genes among functional categories associated with cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin degradation. These comparisons will help determine whether differences in PCWDE abundance and diversity are associated with particular enzyme families or functional categories. However, given the small sample size, these analyses will be purely exploratory, and any statistical results will be interpreted cautiously. 

## Feasibility 
I believe the analyses proposed here are feasible for three reasons. First, I have already selected the ten species and identified their reference genomes and annotation files in GenBank. Second, I have prior experience with Bash scripting (de novo genome assembly and annotation) and R (figure building), which will provide a foundation for using new methods and packages (e.g., dbCAN, pheatmap, ggplot2). Third, I have identified and prepared preliminary scripts for running dbCAN and conducting comparative analyses in R. Extensive online documentation, published workflows on GitHub, and tutorials are also available to help troubleshoot any challenges encountered. These preparations should allow me to complete the HPC3 analyses in a week, and the R analyses within two weeks.  


